\frontsection{Acknowledgement}

We wish to record our sincere gratitude to our supervisor, \supervisorname, and our
co-supervisor, \cosupervisorname, of the Department of Computer Science and Informatics, for
their guidance throughout this research project. Their insistence that every claim be traced
back to a measurement, and their willingness to question results that looked convenient, shaped
this dissertation more than any other single influence.

We thank the Head and the academic staff of the Department of Computer Science and Informatics,
Faculty of Applied Sciences, Uva Wellassa University, for the research project framework, the
proposal and progress review panels, and the feedback given at each stage. The correction of the
fundamental-frequency measurement reported in Section~4.9 was a direct consequence of a panel
question about whether a reported correlation could be trusted.

This study would not have been possible without the \emph{Dialogs Re-enacted Across Languages}
(DRAL) corpus, released into the public domain by Professor Nigel G.\ Ward and colleagues at the
University of Texas at El Paso. A corpus in which the same bilingual speaker performs the same
content in two languages is rare, and it is the instrument on which the central measurement of
this dissertation depends. We are grateful to the research groups who released Whisper, MarianMT,
XTTS-v2, ECAPA-TDNN and SpeechBrain as open models and open source; this project stands on all of
them.

Finally, we thank our families and our colleagues in the 2021 intake for their support and
patience over the course of this work.

\vspace{1.2\baselineskip}
\noindent R.\ Nilakshan\\
